A||||12||Mach10||Attn: Audiophiles!||||6||
Z||000000||Mach10||06-15-2001||09:49 AM||||In the process of mounting a single 10" sealed driver in place of the spare tire up front. I have in-dash phase control for the sub, so placement isn't as much of an issue, but I have it facing rear, towards the passenger compartment. My question(s) is(are):<BR>What can I do to the "psuedo-firewall" to improve sound transmission? Is it worth actually doing anything? The box isn't finished, but I'd rather make any modifications I need to <I>before</I> I bolt the damn thing down.<BR>Has anyone done a similar setup?||||reg||6
Z||000001||Leadfoot||06-15-2001||10:09 AM||||Don't know if this will help but check-out this guys site.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierosound/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierosound/</A> <P>He has done some neat things with the firewall.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||06-15-2001||10:25 AM||||If you go to the link below, there is a guy who installed the sub up front and ran a tube between the trunk and floor.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.crutchfield.com/roadtest/S-ea3vsdSKJYW/daly.asp" TARGET=_blank>http://www.crutchfield.com/roadtest/S-ea3vsdSKJYW/daly.asp</A> <P>Not a bad looking job. I'd like to hear how it sounds. But my guess would be it would sound better than trting ti fire the sub thru the wall and the dash.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||06-15-2001||10:32 AM||||Both of those are the same unit. That's an awesome idea, one that I plan to use in the future, but I am using a completely different setup. That particular setup is called a bandpass box. The subwoofer is completely enclosed in a 2-part box. The rear chamber is sealed, and the front is partially sealed, with 1 or more "vents" or tubes. All of the sound going out comes out the tubes. This type of box must be made to EXACT specs, or else it sounds like garbage. Nice thing about that setup is that you can have the tube coming OUT of the box and into, say, the passenger compartment. That would be nice. <BR>As it is, though, I am running a sealed box, where the driver is exposed. The box is almost done, and I am basically wondering if it's worth it to cut vents through the "firewall" to improve sound transmission.||||reg||7
Z||000004||1FST2M6||06-15-2001||10:50 AM||||why bandpass? they may be loud but they aren't accurate or as crisp and deep as a straight sealed enclouser. a bandpass box is only efficent in a narrow frequency band usually the same Fq as the cars resonant frequency... a single 10 in the floorboard of my 86 hit to 141db... WAY louder than you can stand in the car.. and would hit a Q to a near perfect 35 (40 being perfect) (IASCA measured at Hi-Fi buys Kennesaw, Ga) the dual 4" i had in the dash filtered off an Xtant x-over and amps... you SURE you want a bandpass box? <A HREF="http://www.xtant.com" TARGET=_blank>www.xtant.com</A>  (1001x /lfq45)<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/in-n-out.jpg">   <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstxtant.jpg"> <BR>my amp...<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 06-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||fyerstarter||06-15-2001||12:11 PM||||&lt;homer. Droooll, exxtaaannnnnnt &lt;/homer&gt;<P>Nice amps. Wish I could afford one.||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||06-15-2001||12:26 PM||||I've got 2 1001x's and 2 2100b's i'll sell one of the 1001s with an LFQ shipped for $420.. it's been bench tested at 4v input, 47hz, 12.8v 1121w-rms output @ 1ohm and easily peaks over 2000w. it ran that test for 30min with a 12* increase in temperature.. so it's ROCK SOLID. I could heat up 2ga wire where it was painful to hold with this amp but the amp itself was always cool. polished case and the brushed aluminum case will come with it...  you can't have the new one though..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> i'm too in love... i've got $2100 in recipts for coil upgrades and 3 new rectifiers and the LFQ 45 from xtant for better performance. the amp retails for $1400 with out the upgrades. i really want last years 2000d the only 2000w-rms class D amp made for a 12v applicaion... but at nearly $4000 theres no way in he!!. <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 06-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||06-15-2001||12:37 PM||||Actually, Chuck Daly's box sounds pretty good, for a bandpass. I have one in my 86GT. I bought it a little over a year ago, and am now getting rid of it in favor of a 10" in the cabin. All simulations show 140-142 dB at the dash. Its going to be getting 900w RMS @ 2ohms mono. I'm using an AudioMobile MASS 2010 in a 0.6 cuft box in the cabin. If you want to wait a couple months, or sooner, I am going to get rid of my upfront band pass pretty cheap, so email me if your interested.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||06-15-2001||12:41 PM||||The only way you can get decent sound from a speaker in the front compartment is by using a bandpass box and putting the ports through the floor.<P>Otherwise, your just entertaining the neghborhood, and most of your sound won't get into the cockpit.<P>The situation might be improved somewhat with holes in the floorboard, but most of the sond still won't make it, and you might end up with secondary resonance effects of the front compartment volume with the air in the hole, possibly making a poorly tuned bandpass.<P>There are some tricks to extending the frequency response of a Bandpass.<BR>I built a dual front chamber bandpass with +8 dB efficiency the had a banwidth from 60-200 Hertz, or about an octave higher than a single front chamber box would go.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Seanh||06-15-2001||12:48 PM||||oops. forgot to comment on that. Yeah, thats pretty much right. Your mostly going to be annoying everyone around you, everyone except you in the cabin. I once thought of putting dryer vent tubing from the front compartment to the cabin floorboards, just like Chuck Daly's install, except no bandpass box up there. Then just putting a sealed 10" up there, and letting the bass come thru the tubes on its own. Due to the fact the front chamer is sealed in the fiero, by the weather stip, it may also make for a poorly tuned bandpass like setup. Its also rattles the hell outta your hood if you put a sealed sub up there. Be sure the hood shuts tight. It definatly sounds better then not having any subs, but its not as loud as you'd hope it to be. Since your box is already built, try it and see how you like it for a while. I once comtemplated the idea of four 8" subs in the trunk behind the engine, and might still try it someday in the future just for the heck of it. I'm going to put a different system in each of my Fiero's to experiment with  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||06-15-2001||02:17 PM||||I'm not going for SPL. I just need a little stronger 60hz response. I have 2x10" Kickers in the closet. The sealed box is a temporary solution. Seeing as all I have to deal with is 1/8-1/4" of metal/plastic, vs. 6-8" of foam, plastic, and metal, I cannot see there being much more of a problem than using a trunk.<BR>I happen to like bandpass boxes that have a large passband. Also, you have the ability to "pipe" the sound to wherever you'd like. I.E., a passenger compartment. Yeah, they're loud if you narrow things down, but I like a good range. 50-120hz would be ideal. As for entertaining the neighbors, I wouldn't think that a single 10 will be doing all that much entertaining... <BR>I'm fairly sure that because the remaining area in the trunk is larger than the sub's Vas, then by "porting" the firewall, I shouldn't be getting an appreciable bandpass effect. Besides, there's no ports per se. Cut-out squares approximately 1/8" deep won't be causing much in the way of tuning frequencies... ||||reg||6
Z||000011||Jncomutt||06-16-2001||01:44 AM||||Go check out <A HREF="http://www.subbox.com." TARGET=_blank>www.subbox.com.</A>   Maybe you can pick up some ideas from the things they illustrate.  <P>I have one 10"  Rockford Fosgate HE2 (passenger footwell) powered by a 600watt profile california Amp and its more than enough for what I'm looking for.<P>Also, depending on the year, (and setup) you may want to reverse your wiring to the speakers.  I have an 84 so the speakers are in the headrests.  The way my system is setup, (which is the cheapest way, lol), it requires me to fade to the rear for some extra bass response.  Upon doing this, it is making the headrests too loud, and my 5.25 custom fits in the dash almost unnoticable.  <P>I found when I reversed the wiring and I faded to the rear, it gave that slight imbalace towards the front (which have a lower sensitivity anyway) but yet gave me more bass.  Everything seems even now, and thumps hard....  Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||06-16-2001||11:27 AM||||When you reverse polarity (aka speaker phaze) on a pair of speakers, and not the other pair, they will have a tendancy to cancel out common frequency's. Hence the reason your highs are not as loud to you, and yet you still have good midbass response with the rear (headrest) speakers. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.subbox.com" TARGET=_blank>www.subbox.com</A>  is owned by Chuck Daly, the guys install that was featured on crutchfield in the above link. I have one of those boxes in my 86GT with a 10" Polk woofer in it. It is really loud, and actually sounds descent (for a bandpass) I would still greatly favor in cabin, sealed box over that bandpass. I'm gettin' rid of it here soon, next couple months, so if anyone's interested email me about it. ||||reg||1
